Here is a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <map>
#include <codecvt>

std::string convertMarkdownToHTML(std::string& markdown) {
  std::map<std::string, std::string> markdown_to_html{
      {"# ", "<h1>"}, {"## ", "<h2>"}, {"### ", "<h3>"}, {"#### ", "<h4>"}, {"##### ", "<h5>"}, {"###### ", "<h6>"},  // Headers
      {"\n\n", "</p><p>"},
      {"** ", "<b>"},
      {"* ", "<li>", "</b>"},  // Bold, italic, and bullet points
      {"~ ", "<s>"},
      {"` ", "<code>"},
      {"[ ] ", "<input type='checkbox'"},  // Checkboxes
      {"https?://[^\s]+", "<a href=\"&1\">&1</a>"}  // Hyperlinks
  };

  std::string html;
  std::wstring_convert<std::codecvt_utf8<char>, char> converter;

  for (const auto& [markdown_tag, html_tag] : markdown_to_html) {
    size_t pos = 0;
    while (true) {
      pos = markdown.find(markdown_tag, pos);
      if (pos == std::string::npos) break;
      html += converter.from_bytes(markdown.substr(0, pos)) + html_tag;
      pos += markdown_tag.length();
    }
    html += converter.from_bytes(markdown.substr(pos)) + "</p>";
  }

  return html;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` called `markdown` containing markdown formatted text and iterates through a mapping of markdown tags to corresponding HTML tags. It replaces each markdown tag with its respective HTML tag, applying the replacement one at a time. The function also converts special character sets like URLs to HTML `<a>` tags for hyperlinks. The function returns the resulting HTML-formatted `std::string`.

The `std::wstring_convert` and `codecvt_utf8` headers are used to handle UTF-8 encoded strings during conversion. This allows the function to work with standard C++ strings, which may contain Unicode characters.

