Here's a Java solution to determine if an integer `n` is a power of three without using loops or recursion:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // Negative numbers cannot be powers of 3, as the base (3) is positive
    if (n <= 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // The largest power of 3 that can be represented by a 32-bit integer is 3^19 (6,561,880).
    // If the input number has a factor greater than 6,561,880, it cannot be a power of 3.
    if (n > 6551879) {
        return false;
    }

    // n can only be a power of 3 if it's divisible by 3 with no remainder.
    return (n & (n - 1)) == 0 && (n % 3) == 0;
}
```

This solution has three steps:

1. It checks if `n` is negative, in which case it returns false, as negative numbers cannot be powers of a positive `base` (3 in this case).
2. It checks if `n` is larger than the largest power of 3 that can be represented by a 32-bit integer (3^19 or 6,561,880), in which case it returns false, as no power of 3 can exceed this range.
3. It uses the mathematical fact that a number is a power of 3 if it is divisible by 3 with no remainder. To achieve this, it first checks if `n` is a power of 2 by performing a bitwise AND operation on `n` and `n-1`. If `n` is a power of 2, the resulting value will be zero. Next, it checks if `n` is also divisible by 3 by performing a modulo (remainder) operation with 3. If both conditions are true, it returns true, indicating that `n` is a power of 3.

The algorithm is efficient, as it avoids the overhead of loops or recursion that would otherwise be required to test various exponents of 3.

